  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 245 PM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nSunday...Occasional showers and isolated storms are possible\nthrough the day as an upper trough lingers over the region. \nCooler temperatures are anticipated with highs in the 60s/70s \naccompanied by gusty northeast winds.\n\nSunday Night-Tuesday...The main trough over the Northern \nRockies finally reaches the Northern Plains as low-to-mid-level \nridging over south-central Canada continues to push the surface\ntrough further south into the lower Ohio River Valley. Despite\nthe cold air advection, CVA ahead of the trough keeps scattered\nlight-to-moderate showers (50-70% coverage) around Sunday night\ninto Monday. By Monday night, the trough finally reaches Iowa \nas cold-core showers form underneath, keeping PoPs around \nthrough Tuesday morning. As subsidence sets in on the backside \nof the trough and high pressure builds in from the north, the \noutlook area should be rain-free by Tuesday evening.\n\nWednesday-Thursday...Fall-like weather is likely (70-90%) to \nreturn for the middle of next week. Confluence aloft and surface\nhigh pressure is forecasted to keep Wednesday and Thursday dry \nas CAA keeps temperatures below average with highs in the \nmid-60s to low 70s and lows in the mid-40s to low 50s.",
